    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n2940" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ānus</span></b> , i, m.
		for as-nus; cf. Sanscr. ās, = to sit, seat one's self;
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἧμαι</span> (Dor. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἧσμαι</span>) <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κάθ-ημαι</span>, Varr.; others refer it to 2. anus, from its
			form, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>the
				posteriors</i>, <i>fundament.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>, * <i><span class="en">Cic. Fam. 9,
				22</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Cels. 7, 30</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Scrib.
				Comp. 227</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Meton.</b>, disease of the anus, <i>piles</i>, <i>hemorrhoids</i> (eccl.  Lat.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">quinque anos aureos facietis, i. e.
					representations of,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Vulg. 1
					Reg. 6, 5</span></i>
			
			<i>bis;</i> 6, 11; 6, 17.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
